One more data point and the change I'm running locally. It keeps a per-watcher set of files already added, so each out-of-root file is registered once, and it drops a file from the set when chokidar reports it deleted so a recreated file is watched again:

```ts
const ensuredWatchFiles = new WeakMap<FSWatcher, Set<string>>()

export function ensureWatchedFile(watcher: FSWatcher, file: string | null, root: string): void {
  if (!file || normalizePath(file).startsWith(withTrailingSlash(root)) || file.includes('\0')) return

  let ensured = ensuredWatchFiles.get(watcher)
  if (!ensured) {
    const files = new Set<string>()
    watcher.on('unlink', (unlinked) => files.delete(path.resolve(unlinked)))
    ensuredWatchFiles.set(watcher, files)
    ensured = files
  }

  const resolved = path.resolve(file)
  if (ensured.has(resolved) || !fs.existsSync(file)) return

  ensured.add(resolved)
  watcher.add(resolved)
}
```

On 8.3.1 with this change, transforming one out-of-root module repeatedly leaves one entry for it in `server.watcher._closers`.

This comes from Lightning CSS, which Vite 8 uses as the default CSS minifier, not from Vite itself. I reproduced it with Vite 8.3.5 / lightningcss 1.33.0:

```js
transform({ code: Buffer.from('a{border-image-outset:0 0px}'), minify: true })
// a{border-image-outset:0 0}
```

The usual "`0px` → `0`" shortening is safe for properties that only take `<length>`. `border-image-outset` (and `border-image-width`) take `<length> | <number>` though, and a bare `0` parses as the *number* 0, which means "0 × border-width". For static styles you get the same result. In an animation it isn't the same, because a length and a number can't be interpolated, so the keyframes become discrete. I checked in Chrome 156 by pausing the animation at 25%:

| `to` keyframe | computed `border-image-outset` at 25% |
|---|---|
| `0 0px` (dev) | `0 150px` |
| `0 0` (after build) | `0 200px` (no interpolation) |

Workarounds that I tested in a build:

1. Switch the CSS minifier to esbuild (needs `esbuild` installed as a dev dependency), which keeps `0px`:
   ```js
   // vite.config.js
   export default { build: { cssMinify: 'esbuild' } }
   ```
2. Or hide the unit from the minifier with a custom property. Lightning CSS leaves custom properties alone, and in Chrome this interpolates normally (`0 150px` at 25%):
   ```css
   :root { --zero: 0px; }
   @keyframes donateBorder {
     from { border-image-outset: 0 200px; }
     to   { border-image-outset: 0 var(--zero); }
   }
   ```

`calc(0px)` doesn't help, because it gets folded to `0` too.

The real fix should go to parcel-bundler/lightningcss. `LengthOrNumber` (src/values/length.rs) serializes `Length(0px)` the same way as `Number(0)`, so it needs to keep the unit when the value is a zero length. I haven't built that change, so I've only tested the two workarounds above.

Hey @rossng  @szupzj18 ! Just checking if either of you is actively working on this?
From what I can tell in bundleWorkerEntry, the cache only tracks finished bundles, which is why overlapping requests for the same nested worker trigger duplicate builds.
@rossng, if you're already on the PR, I'm glad to just review/test. If not, I can take it on and open one with a regression test included.

